  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe strategy employs an iterative cutting-plane method to minimize the maximum overlap by sequentially addressing the most problematic lags. Each iteration involves solving an optimization problem to tighten constraints on the worst lag, ensuring feasibility and improving the solution incrementally. This method systematically reduces the maximum overlap by focusing on critical points, potentially escaping local minima and converging to a tighter upper bound.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nObjective: Minimize C such that for all lags k,  \n\u03a3(h[i] * (1 - h[i + k])) * dx \u2264 C.  \n\nConstraints:  \n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1  \n- \u03a3 h[i] = n_points / 2  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \na. Initialize h from `initial_h_values`, normalize to sum(h) = n_points / 2.  \nb. For each iteration:  \n   i. Compute all overlaps to find the current maximum C and the worst lag k.  \n   ii. Formulate and solve an optimization problem to minimize C with a constraint for lag k.  \n   iii. Update h, ensuring feasibility.  \nc. Terminate when C stops improving or time budget is exhausted.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n- Population size: 30  \n- Mutation rate: 0.05 (per element)  \n- Crossover rate: 0.7  \n- SLSQP iterations: 100 per candidate  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \nEstimated 300\u2013500s for processing with checks for improvement and feasibility.\n\n6. Validation and return contract  \nEnsure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) = n_points / 2. Re-evaluate C\u2085 with the actual task metric before returning.",
